## std::scalar — scalar primitive method-homes (Int, Float, Bool, Char, USize).
##
## Zero-Rust-stdlib migration, Phase 3 (Option C) + Feature A. The classes
## below are the METHOD-HOME classes for the scalar primitive heads: each
## carries the FFI alias decls (`Int_to_s` → `ruxen_int_to_string`, etc.)
## AND the true surface return types delegated via the builtin-receiver
## bridge (`bridge_builtin_method`, reached because
## `builtin_bridge::is_delegated_head` includes the scalar heads).
##
## CRITICAL INVARIANT — these classes change only the METHOD-HOME, NOT the
## value representation. Each is registered as `TypeAlias { target: <head> }`
## by `register_primitives`; the bootstrap merge anchors the class onto the
## same DefId and stomps it to `Class`, but `resolve_type_expr`'s
## `primitive_class_ty` fast-path returns the primitive head for a bare
## `let x: Int` annotation, so literals, arithmetic, comparisons, and the C
## ABI / runtime repr are unchanged. The receiver value is passed by value
## at the call site — `42.to_s`, `'a'.to_s`.
##
## ## ABI notes — the FFI receiver width
##
## The instance-method receiver is prepended to the derived FFI param list
## by `primitive_ffi_receiver_ty` (`register_class_lib_method_in`), choosing
## a `Ty` whose lowered width matches the C symbol's first parameter:
##
##   * `Float` → `Ty::Float` (F64) — its `ruxen_float_*` symbols take a
##     `double`.
##   * `Int` / `USize` / `Bool` / `Char` → an explicit `Ty::Int64` — their
##     C symbols take the value by value in an `int64_t` slot
##     (`ruxen_int_to_string(int64_t)`, `ruxen_bool_to_string(int64_t)`,
##     `ruxen_char_to_string(int64_t)`). `Ty::Int64` lowers to i64 on EVERY
##     backend/target. A `Ty::Class` receiver also lowered to I64 in
##     Cranelift and on 64-bit LLVM (ptr == i64), so it matched there — but
##     the LLVM backend lowers `Ty::Class` to a real `ptr`, which is **i32
##     on wasm32**, producing an `(i32) -> i32` decl that clashed with the
##     canonical `(i64)` runtime decl and made wasm-ld splice a trapping
##     stub. The Bool/Char receiver value (i8 / i32) is zero-extended to i64
##     by `coerce_value` at the call site (both are non-negative).
##
## The parity guard `tests/runtime_abi_derivation.rs` pins every derived
## width against the frozen `runtime_signature` table.
class Int
  # `Add` capability: Int elements can be `sum`med (receiver-element
  # bound on `Array[T].sum where T: Add`). Nominal opt-in — the bound
  # check requires an explicit `include`.
  include Add

  lib "../../string/runtime/string.c"
    def to_string as "ruxen_int_to_string" -> String
    def to_s as "ruxen_int_to_string" -> String
    def to_f as "ruxen_int_to_f" -> Float
  end
end

## Feature A — scalar method-homes for Float / Bool / Char / USize.
##
## These migrated once `primitive_ffi_receiver_ty` taught the receiver-
## prepend to use the C symbol's TRUE register class:
##   * `Float` → F64 (its `ruxen_float_*` symbols take a `double`);
##   * `Int` / `USize` / `Bool` / `Char` → an explicit `Ty::Int64` (their
##     `ruxen_*` symbols take the value by-value in an `int64_t` slot).
##     A `Ty::Class` receiver lowered to I64 in Cranelift AND on 64-bit
##     LLVM (ptr == i64), so it matched THERE — but the LLVM backend
##     lowers `Ty::Class` to a real `ptr`, which is **i32 on wasm32**,
##     producing an `(i32) -> i32` decl that clashed with the canonical
##     `(i64)` runtime decl and made wasm-ld splice a trapping stub. The
##     explicit `Ty::Int64` is i64 on every backend/target. The
##     migration is the `.rx` shell + `primitive_class_ty` +
##     `is_delegated_head` entries; width is i64 everywhere now.
## The parity guard `tests/runtime_abi_derivation.rs` pins every derived
## width against the frozen `runtime_signature` table.
class Float
  # `Add` capability — see `class Int`.
  include Add

  lib "../../string/runtime/string.c"
    def to_string as "ruxen_float_to_string" -> String
    def to_s as "ruxen_float_to_string" -> String
    def to_i as "ruxen_float_to_i" -> Int
  end
end

class Bool
  lib "../../string/runtime/string.c"
    def to_string as "ruxen_bool_to_string" -> String
    def to_s as "ruxen_bool_to_string" -> String
  end
end

class Char
  lib "../../string/runtime/string.c"
    def to_s as "ruxen_char_to_string" -> String
    def to_string as "ruxen_char_to_string" -> String
  end
end

class USize
  # `Add` capability — see `class Int`.
  include Add

  lib "../../string/runtime/string.c"
    def to_string as "ruxen_int_to_string" -> String
    def to_s as "ruxen_int_to_string" -> String
  end
end
